#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084397 :tutorial/content "A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {10}\n$$\n\nFor example, the number $0.\\overline{8}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.8^(―) &= 0.8 + 0.08 + 0.008 + … \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{10} + \\frac{8}{100} + \\frac{8}{1000} + …\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.8^(―) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{1}r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{8}{10}(\\frac{1}{10})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8(\\frac{1}{10})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3998b477-1c28-5edc-8605-2738ce474d94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1212 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
